# ============================================================================
# FlowAblation / stagnation-point heating of a cylinder in a Mach 3 stream  (Hydro2, ablation.on = 1)
#
# The wedge free stream (rho 1, p 0.714286, U 3 -> a = 1, Mach 3; gamma 1.4, cp 1005, R = 287.14,
# T_inf = 2.4876e-3) on a cylinder of radius 0.25 at the origin, cold wall T_w = T_inf, mu = 2e-3
# (Re_D = 750), Pr 0.72.  ablation.Qstar is huge: the surface does not move, the test is the heat flux.
# References (reference/blunt_body_check.py):
#   * stagnation pressure = Rayleigh pitot, p0_2/p_inf = 12.061          (exact, inviscid)
#   * shock stand-off  Delta/R = 0.386 exp(4.67/M^2) = 0.648             (Billig correlation)
#   * stagnation heat flux, Fay-Riddell for a 2D body, constant mu, Le = 1:
#       q = 0.57 Pr^-0.6 (rho_e mu)^0.4 (rho_w mu)^0.1 sqrt(beta) cp (T_0 - T_w),
#       beta = (1/R) sqrt(2 (p_e - p_inf)/rho_e)   (Newtonian velocity gradient)
#     a boundary-layer correlation, good to ~10 % at this Reynolds number.
# Only the FRONT half is in the box (outflow at x = 0, supersonic outside the boundary layer there): the
# low-density wake (rho ~ 0.1) would cut the viscous time step by 10.
# Boundary-layer scale sqrt(mu/(rho_e beta)) = 0.0078 = 3 cells of dx = 0.0025: UNDER-RESOLVED (thermal layer ~7 cells).
# OPEN BUG (2026-10-05): with mu = 1e-2 (7 cells per scale) the run blows up at t = 0.0078: the temperature of the
# second fluid cell on the stagnation line grows exponentially (T/T_inf 2.3 -> 7 in 0.003) until the HLLC solver
# fails.  Same instant for cfl_v 0.3 .. 0.15 (not a time-step limit), with solid.visc_mirror 0 or 1, on every binary
# since v24; does not happen with an adiabatic wall (solid.T_wall = 0) or with mu = 2e-3.
#
# Run (from bin/): mpirun -np 8 ./hydro2-2d-g++ ../tests/FlowAblation/input_2D_BluntBody plot_file=<dir>/output
# Check:           python3 ../tests/FlowAblation/reference/blunt_body_check.py <dir>
# ============================================================================
